WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.10 · Financial Management
Financial health and sustainability

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Judge financial health by resilience and dependency, not by the size of your first grant.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.652
Financial sustainability means being able to fund your mission over time, cope with shocks and invest in the future. You judge it by financial health, not by how fast your income grows.

4
00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:27.652
Four health measures

5
00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:38.981
Reserves and runway: Months of unrestricted reserves, and months you could run without new income. For example: The bakery holds one month of reserves and aims for three within two years.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:50.311
Dependency: The share of your income that comes from your largest single source. For example: One grant is 70% of the youth club’s income; the target is under 50%.

7
00:00:50.311 --> 00:01:00.530
Flexible income: The share of unrestricted income you can use for core costs. For example: Sales and memberships give the repair café 40% flexible income this year.

8
00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:11.948
Full cost recovery: Whether your projects cover their fair share of shared costs. For example: The tutoring pilot covers 80% of its full cost; the target is 100%.

9
00:01:12.348 --> 00:01:12.948
Why health beats size

10
00:01:12.948 --> 00:01:18.622
1. A big first grant or contract can hide a fragile model.

11
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:23.926
2. Weak starting values are normal; the trend is what matters.

12
00:01:23.926 --> 00:01:30.600
3. They warn you before dependency or low reserves become a crisis.

13
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.600
How you will use them

14
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.274
1. In your annual review, to see whether your funding mix works.

15
00:01:37.274 --> 00:01:43.319
2. To choose the income mix that suits the form you are building.

16
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:49.252
3. To set targets for reserves, dependency and flexible income.

17
00:01:49.652 --> 00:01:50.252
What to avoid

18
00:01:50.252 --> 00:01:54.815
Celebrating income growth while reserves and runway shrink.

19
00:01:54.815 --> 00:02:00.119
Tracking so many measures that none of them gets used.

20
00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:06.052
Setting no targets because your starting values look weak.

21
00:02:06.652 --> 00:02:08.452
You choose your health measures, starting values and targets.

22
00:02:08.452 --> 00:02:12.452
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:13.952 --> 00:02:17.052
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
